("p", "The x-axis and y-axis meet at the **origin (0, 0)** and split the plane into four quadrants. A point is written (x, y); x is the **abscissa** and y the **ordinate**. A point on the x-axis is (x, 0); a point on the y-axis is (0, y)."),
("table", ["Quadrant", "Signs", "Example"], [["I", "(+, +)", "(3, 4)"], ["II", "(&minus;, +)", "(&minus;3, 4)"], ["III", "(&minus;, &minus;)", "(&minus;3, &minus;4)"], ["IV", "(+, &minus;)", "(3, &minus;4)"]]),
("h2", "7.2 Distance Formula"),
("note", "FORMULA", "The distance between P(x<sub>1</sub>, y<sub>1</sub>) and Q(x<sub>2</sub>, y<sub>2</sub>) is  **PQ = &radic;[(x<sub>2</sub> &minus; x<sub>1</sub>)<sup>2</sup> + (y<sub>2</sub> &minus; y<sub>1</sub>)<sup>2</sup>].**  The distance of P(x, y) from the origin is **&radic;(x<sup>2</sup> + y<sup>2</sup>)**."),
("p", "The formula comes from the Pythagoras theorem applied to the right triangle with PQ as hypotenuse and horizontal and vertical legs |x<sub>2</sub> &minus; x<sub>1</sub>| and |y<sub>2</sub> &minus; y<sub>1</sub>|."),
("p", "**Examples.** Distance between (2, 3) and (4, 1) is &radic;(4 + 4) = **2&radic;2**. Distance from (&minus;5, 7) to (0, 0) is &radic;(25 + 49) = &radic;74. **Point on the x-axis equidistant from (2, &minus;5) and (&minus;2, 9):** let it be (x, 0); (x &minus; 2)<sup>2</sup> + 25 = (x + 2)<sup>2</sup> + 81 gives &minus;8x = 56, so **x = &minus;7**, point (&minus;7, 0)."),
("h3", "Using the distance formula to identify figures"),
("table", ["To show that the points form", "Prove that"], [["Collinear points", "The sum of two distances equals the third (AB + BC = AC)"], ["Isosceles triangle", "Two sides equal"], ["Equilateral triangle", "All three sides equal"], ["Right triangle", "Sum of squares of two sides = square of the third"], ["Parallelogram", "Opposite sides equal (or diagonals bisect each other)"], ["Rhombus", "All four sides equal"], ["Rectangle", "Opposite sides equal and diagonals equal"], ["Square", "Four sides equal and diagonals equal"]]),
("p", "**Example.** A(1, 2), B(3, 4), C(5, 6): AB = BC = 2&radic;2 and AC = 4&radic;2 = AB + BC, so they are **collinear**. **Square:** (1, 7), (4, 2), (&minus;1, &minus;1), (&minus;4, 4) have all sides &radic;34 and both diagonals &radic;68, so the figure is a **square**. **Centre of circle:** if (2, &minus;3) and (&minus;4, 5) are ends of a diameter, the centre is (&minus;1, 1) and the radius is 5."),
("h2", "7.3 Section Formula"),
("note", "FORMULA", "The point P dividing the segment joining A(x<sub>1</sub>, y<sub>1</sub>) and B(x<sub>2</sub>, y<sub>2</sub>) **internally in the ratio m : n** is  **P = ( (m x<sub>2</sub> + n x<sub>1</sub>)/(m + n), (m y<sub>2</sub> + n y<sub>1</sub>)/(m + n) ).**  **Mid-point (m = n):** ((x<sub>1</sub> + x<sub>2</sub>)/2, (y<sub>1</sub> + y<sub>2</sub>)/2)."),
("p", "**Example.** Point dividing (&minus;3, 10) and (6, &minus;8) in the ratio 2 : 1 is ((12 &minus; 3)/3, (&minus;16 + 10)/3) = **(3, &minus;2)**. **Points of trisection** of (4, &minus;1) and (&minus;2, &minus;3): ratios 1 : 2 and 2 : 1 give (2, &minus;5/3) and (0, &minus;7/3)."),
("steps", [("Find the ratio in which a line divides a segment", "Take the ratio as k : 1 (or m : n), apply the section formula to the coordinate that is known (e.g. y = 0 for the x-axis) and solve for k."), ("Example", "The x-axis divides (2, &minus;3) and (5, 6): y = (6k &minus; 3)/(k + 1) = 0 gives k = 1/2, so the ratio is **1 : 2** and the point is **(3, 0)**."), ("Finding the fourth vertex", "In a parallelogram the diagonals bisect each other: equate the mid-points of the two diagonals.")]),
("p", "**Example (parallelogram).** Vertices A(1, 2), B(4, 3), C(6, 6) and D(x, y): mid-point of AC = (3.5, 4) equals mid-point of BD = ((4 + x)/2, (3 + y)/2), so **D = (3, 5)**."),
